{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Despite their importance and wide application, the quality of meta-analyses is highly variable [1,7,10,11]. If the quality of meta-analyses is poor, it is hard to know if 'biological metaanalysis embodies 'mega-enlightenment', a 'mega-mistake', or something in between' [12]. One issue that can prevent readers from evaluating the overall quality of a published metaanalysis is the lack of details describing each step in the meta-analysis. We refer to this as reporting quality, which is the degree to which the meta-analysis explicitly reports the steps taken to conduct the meta-analysis, including details about the methods used to identify studies and extract data, the meta-analytic model, the number of effect sizes, and the sources of non-independence [7].",
      "reason": "The Introduction describes the current state of knowledge about meta-analyses in ecology, highlights variability in quality, and identifies key uncertainties/issues (e.g., poor reporting, lack of detail, non-independence) that motivate evaluation."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Assessing the current compliance with recommended steps for reporting and execution in ecological meta-analyses and identifying places that need improvements can help guide the meta-analytic community towards more robust inference and reduce controversy. In this paper, we reviewed the literature assessing the quality of ecological meta-analyses, collected new data to evaluate current practices, and highlight the areas that need more work.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly explains the importance and need for the review — to assess compliance, identify areas for improvement, and guide the community toward more robust inference."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "We also re-evaluated all the meta-analyses available from Pappalardo et al. [1] to extract new information on ten additional criteria and to assess how the meta-analyses recognized and addressed non-independence.",
      "reason": "The paper acknowledges previous similar work (cites Pappalardo et al. and other review papers), explains the relationship to that prior review, and describes why and how they re-evaluated or updated those earlier results."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "To evaluate current practices when conducting and reporting ecological meta-analyses, we surveyed the literature for quantitative assessments on criteria previously identified as best practices in meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The review addresses general reporting and execution practices in ecological meta-analyses rather than reviews of intervention effects; therefore a description of an intervention's mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "To evaluate current practices when conducting and reporting ecological meta-analyses, we surveyed the literature for quantitative assessments on criteria previously identified as best practices in meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The review is not focused on complex interventions or delivery contexts and does not propose or require a logic model; thus including a logic model is not applicable to this manuscript's scope."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "In this paper, we reviewed the literature assessing the quality of ecological meta-analyses, collected new data to evaluate current practices, and highlight the areas that need more work. First, we compiled information from 18 studies in the last 20 years (between 2002 and 2022) that reviewed the quality of meta-analyses in ecology, evolution and related fields. ... Second, we evaluated the recognition and treatment of non-independence for the ecological meta-analyses included in Pappalardo et al. [1], and extracted new data on quality criteria to compare it with the other reviews. Finally, we summarized the level of compliance for different quality criteria across these 18 previous meta-analysis reviews and the new data taken from papers reviewed in Pappalardo et al [1].",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the objectives of the review (to review literature on quality of ecological meta-analyses, collect new data, evaluate treatment of non-independence, and summarize compliance). However, it does not present those objectives using a formal question‑formulation framework (e.g., PICO or a similar structured framework), so the requirement is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "In this paper, we reviewed the literature assessing the quality of ecological meta-analyses, collected new data to evaluate current practices, and highlight the areas that need more work.",
      "reason": "This review is a meta-evaluation of reporting and execution quality across ecological meta-analyses rather than a review of intervention effects requiring planned comparisons; therefore the PICO-style requirement for planned intervention comparisons does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Of the 61 papers for which we screened the full text, the majority were excluded for not having quantitative data on the selected criteria (Fig 1). One paper was excluded for being out of scope. Two of the papers [14,15] overlapped considerably... To reduce non-independence in our compilation, we kept only [15] that had information for six of our selected criteria; i.e., we excluded [14] from our analyses.",
      "reason": "The methods and Table 2 describe inclusion/exclusion screening and note exclusion reasons (e.g., lacking quantitative data, out of scope) and the PRISMA flow is provided, but the manuscript does not fully specify all study-characteristic eligibility elements (e.g., specific eligible study designs, settings, or follow-up requirements) in a comprehensive list."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "We used a search string for TOPIC as: (['meta-analyses' OR 'metaanalyses' OR 'meta analyses'] AND ['quality' OR 'performance criteria'] AND ['reporting']). The search resulted in 751 citations... The 765 papers that were obtained were then screened using the metagear [13] R package... this set was reduced to 61 papers.",
      "reason": "The manuscript reports search dates, databases searched, and time spans and indicates the time periods covered by review papers (Table 1), but it does not explicitly state eligibility restrictions on report characteristics such as language or publication/report status; thus some report-characteristic criteria are described (time period/search), but not fully specified."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": "text_12",
      "quote": "Meta-analysis papers were less consistent in their reporting of information that can critically affect the results of a meta-analysis (e.g., the inclusion/exclusion criteria [49]).",
      "reason": "There is discussion that some meta-analyses failed to report outcomes or data, but the manuscript does not explicitly distinguish exclusion because outcomes were not measured versus exclusion because outcome results were not reported when describing eligibility criteria for studies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Our final list of criteria for analysis is presented in Table 2, where we detail which paper contributed data to each criterion. Because different review papers used slightly different criteria (or different names to refer to similar criteria), we matched similar criteria and provided details on which information was extracted for each review paper in S3 Appendix.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly specify the groups/criteria used for their syntheses (Table 2) and indicate which review papers contributed to each criterion, showing how studies were grouped by criterion. However, they do not explicitly link those groups to specific comparisons defined in the review objectives, so the requirement is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Of the 61 papers for which we screened the full text, the majority were excluded for not having quantitative data on the selected criteria (Fig 1). One paper was excluded for being out of scope. ... To reduce non-independence in our compilation, we kept only [15] that had information for six of our selected criteria; i.e., we excluded [14] from our analyses.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides explicit rationales for notable restrictions or exclusions (e.g., excluding papers that did not provide quantitative data, excluding out-of-scope papers, and excluding overlapping datasets to reduce non-independence), with these reasons reported in the methods and PRISMA flow."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "70"
